      "patternHeading": "What the verses are doing",
      "patternText": "ὁ λόγος σὰρξ ἐγένετο, the Word became flesh: both nouns stay nominative with ἐγένετο, as with ἦν. ἐσκήνωσεν, he dwelt, is a first aorist; the σ is the aorist sign, and the stem vowel is lengthened, as Harper notes (σκηνο- to ἐσκήνωσε-). ἐθεασάμεθα, we beheld, is a first aorist middle of the deponent θέαομαι. δόξαν ὡς μονογενοῦς παρὰ πατρός, glory as of an only-begotten from a father: μονογενοῦς is the genitive of μονογενής, an adjective of two endings, and -έος contracts to -οῦς with a circumflex. πατρός is for πατέρος, a short vowel dropped between two consonants. πλήρης χάριτος καὶ ἀληθείας, full of grace and of truth. κέκραγεν is a perfect, he has cried, used with present meaning; λέγων, saying, is a present participle. This lesson takes the verse through λέγων.",
