      "patternText": "The lesson picks up after λέγων. ὁ εἰπών, he who said, and ὁ ἐρχόμενος, he who comes, show Harper’s rule: with the article, the participle is equivalent to a relative clause. ὀπίσω μου, after me, and ἔμπροσθέν μου, before me, use the genitive; μου is enclitic and loses its own accent, and ἔμπροσθεν and πρῶτος each take a second acute because they stand before that enclitic. ὅτι, because. ἐκ τοῦ πληρώματος αὐτοῦ ἡμεῖς πάντες ἐλάβομεν, from his fullness we all received, and χάριν ἀντὶ χάριτος: χάριν is the accusative of χάρις (Harper notes the third-declension accusative in -ν rather than χάριτα), and ἀντί always takes the genitive. ὁ νόμος διὰ Μωϋσέως ἐδόθη, the law was given through Moses: ἐδόθη is a first aorist passive of δίδωμι. ἡ χάρις καὶ ἡ ἀλήθεια διὰ Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ ἐγένετο, grace and truth came through Jesus Christ. Harper notes that the genitive of Moses ends in -έως, and that Ἰησοῦς is the only proper noun of the second declension ending in -οῦς.",
