      "patternHeading": "What the verses are doing",
      "patternText": "John testified, ἐμαρτύρησεν, a first aorist of the pure (vowel) verb μαρτυρέω: the short ε of the stem is lengthened before -σα, and ε- is prefixed as the syllabic augment. τεθέαμαι, I have beheld, is a perfect: τε- is reduplication, the sign of completed action, and the rough θ of θεάομαι becomes smooth τ in the reduplication. The Spirit is seen καταβαῖνον ὡς περιστερὰν ἐξ οὐρανοῦ, descending like a dove out of heaven, and ἔμεινεν ἐπ᾽ αὐτόν, it remained on him: ἔμεινεν is a liquid first aorist of μένω, which rejects σ and lengthens the stem vowel. The one who sent John said, ἐφ᾽ ὃν ἂν ἴδῃς τὸ πνεῦμα καταβαῖνον καὶ μένον ἐπ᾽ αὐτόν, οὗτός ἐστιν ὁ βαπτίζων ἐν πνεύματι ἁγίῳ. After a copulative verb such as ἐστίν, the predicate stays in the same case as the subject: οὗτός ἐστιν ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ θεοῦ, this one is the Son of God.",
      "patternRows": [
        [
          "τεθέαμαι τὸ πνεῦμα καταβαῖνον ὡς περιστερὰν ἐξ οὐρανοῦ",
          "I have seen the Spirit descending like a dove out of heaven."
        ],
        [
          "ἔμεινεν ἐπ᾽ αὐτόν",
          "It remained on him. (liquid first aorist of μένω)"
        ],
        [
          "οὗτός ἐστιν ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ θεοῦ",
          "This is the Son of God. (subject and predicate both nominative)"
        ]
      ],
      "rules": [
        {
          "id": "l15-aorist",
          "title": "The first aorist of a vowel verb adds -σα, and lengthens a short final vowel",
          "rule": "The aorist represents an action as simply brought to pass. In the indicative the sign of past time is the augment. The first-aorist active stem is made by adding -σα to the theme. In a vowel (pure) verb, a short vowel at the end of the theme becomes long before σ: μαρτυρέω gives ἐμαρτύρησεν, he testified.",
          "example": "καὶ ἐμαρτύρησεν Ἰωάννης λέγων",
          "notThis": "ἐμαρτύρεσεν, without lengthening ε to η before σ",
          "useThis": "ἐμαρτύρησεν, he testified",
          "because": "Harper’s observations 3–7 on the aorist and on vowel verbs."
        },
        {
          "id": "l15-liquid",
          "title": "A liquid first aorist rejects σ and lengthens the stem vowel",
          "rule": "Liquid verbs (stems ending in λ, μ, ν, or ρ) reject σ in the first aorist and lengthen the theme-vowel in compensation. μένω, I remain, has the first aorist ἔμεινεν, it remained, not ἔμενσεν.",
          "example": "καὶ ἔμεινεν ἐπ᾽ αὐτόν",
          "notThis": "ἔμενσεν, as if the liquid verb kept the σ of -σα",
          "useThis": "ἔμεινεν, it remained",
          "because": "Harper’s observation 8, and the note on ἔμεινεν in this lesson."
        },
        {
          "id": "l15-neuterpl",
          "title": "A neuter plural subject often takes a singular verb",
          "rule": "A neuter plural nominative often takes a singular verb. You have already read πάντα δι᾽ αὐτοῦ ἐγένετο, all things came to be through him, and ταῦτα ἐν Βηθανίᾳ ἐγένετο, these things happened in Bethany: πάντα and ταῦτα are plural, ἐγένετο is singular.",
          "example": "ταῦτα ἐν Βηθανίᾳ ἐγένετο πέραν τοῦ Ἰορδάνου",
          "notThis": "ταῦτα ἐγένοντο, forcing a plural verb as English would",
          "useThis": "ταῦτα ἐγένετο, these things happened (singular verb)",
          "because": "Harper’s Principle 5. The examples are John 1:3 and 1:28."
        },
        {
          "id": "l15-pred",
          "title": "After a copulative verb, the predicate noun stays in the same case as the subject",
          "rule": "After verbs such as εἰμί and γίνομαι, the predicate noun is in the same case as the subject. Both οὗτος and ὁ υἱός are nominative: this one is the Son of God. So also θεὸς ἦν ὁ λόγος, and ἐγὼ οὐκ εἰμὶ ὁ χριστός.",
          "example": "οὗτός ἐστιν ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ θεοῦ",
          "notThis": "οὗτός ἐστιν τὸν υἱόν, putting the predicate in the accusative",
          "useThis": "οὗτός ἐστιν ὁ υἱός, both nominative",
          "because": "Harper’s Principle 6: after copulative verbs the predicate noun is in the same case as the subject."
        }
      ],
