      "patternHeading": "What the verses are doing",
      "patternText": "There was a man of the Pharisees, Nicodemus, a ruler of the Jews. He came to Jesus by night, νυκτός, and said, ῥαββί, οἴδαμεν ὅτι ἀπὸ θεοῦ ἐλήλυθας διδάσκαλος: we know that you have come from God as a teacher — ἐλήλυθας is the perfect of ἔρχομαι. No one can do these signs ἃ σὺ ποιεῖς, which you are doing: the relative ἅ agrees with σημεῖα in gender and number, but its case is the object case of ποιεῖς, the structure of its own clause. Jesus answers, ἐὰν μή τις γεννηθῇ ἄνωθεν, unless one is born from above (or anew), he cannot see the kingdom of God. Nicodemus asks how a man, being old, can be born a second time. Jesus says he must be born of water and Spirit, and μὴ θαυμάσῃς, do not marvel: the negative with an imperative or a subjunctive is always μή, not οὐ. δεῖ ὑμᾶς γεννηθῆναι ἄνωθεν, you must be born from above. The wind blows where it wills; so is everyone who has been born of the Spirit. Nicodemus asks, πῶς δύναται ταῦτα γενέσθαι; and Jesus says, you are the teacher of Israel, and you do not know these things?",
