      "patternHeading": "What the verses are doing",
      "patternText": "The woman asks for the water so that she may not thirst or come here to draw. Jesus tells her to call her husband; she has had five, and the one she has now is not her husband. She perceives that he is a prophet, and raises the old question of the mountain and Jerusalem. Jesus says the hour is coming, and now is, when the true worshipers will worship the Father ἐν πνεύματι καὶ ἀληθείᾳ. πνεῦμα ὁ θεός, God is spirit; those who worship him must worship in spirit and truth. ἡ σωτηρία ἐκ τῶν Ἰουδαίων ἐστίν: the article with an abstract noun, ἡ σωτηρία, is often left untranslated in English, “salvation is from the Jews.” The woman speaks of the Messiah; Jesus says, ἐγώ εἰμι, ὁ λαλῶν σοι, I who speak to you am he. The disciples marvel that he was speaking μετὰ γυναικός, with a woman — μετά with the genitive means with, in company with; with the accusative it means after, as in μετὰ ταῦτα. She leaves her jar, calls the town, δεῦτε ἴδετε, Come, see; μήτι οὗτός ἐστιν ὁ χριστός; This couldn’t be the Christ, could it? — μήτι also looks toward “no,” or at least holds the claim in question. Meanwhile the disciples urge him to eat; his food is to do the will of the one who sent him and to finish his work. The fields are white for harvest. Many Samaritans believe, first because of the woman’s word, then because they have heard him themselves, and they know that this is truly the Savior of the world. Vowel verbs contract in the imperfect as in the present (ἐλάλει, ἠρώτων); their future generally lengthens a short final vowel (προσκυνήσετε). Liquid futures add ε in place of σ, and then contract (ἀναγγελεῖ).",
