          "id": "l38-susp",
          "title": "A nominative may stand apart for emphasis (suspended nominative)",
          "rule": "In some passages a nominative is found unconnected with the grammatical structure of the sentence. This is known as a suspended nominative, and denotes emphasis. Harper points to examples such as 7:38 and 8:45 (OCR of the references is a little worn; the construction is the hanging nominative you can already feel in a line that names someone, then starts the sentence afresh).",
          "example": "πρὶν Ἀβραὰμ γενέσθαι ἐγὼ εἰμί",
          "notThis": "forcing every nominative to be the ordinary subject of the nearest verb, and losing the emphatic hanging name when it occurs",
          "useThis": "a nominative set apart for emphasis, then the sentence proper",
          "because": "Harper’s observation 6. The verse references in the OCR are doubtful; the description of the construction is not."
