      "rules": [
        {
          "id": "l40-comp-an",
          "title": "The comparative takes a genitive or ἤ; clauses with ἄν take the subjunctive",
          "rule": "μείζονα τούτων, greater than these. The comparative usually takes the object of comparison in the genitive, or it may be followed by ἤ. Relative, conditional, and temporal clauses that contain ἄν are followed by the subjunctive: ἃ γὰρ ἂν ἐκεῖνος ποιῇ.",
          "example": "μείζονα τούτων δείξει αὐτῷ ἔργα",
          "notThis": "writing an indicative after ἄν, or treating τούτων as a mere object without comparison",
          "useThis": "greater than these; whatever he does (ἂν … ποιῇ)",
          "because": "Principles 32 and 33, Lessons XXXII."
        },
        {
          "id": "l40-cond",
          "title": "Four conditions, and contrary to fact in full",
          "rule": "You have seen a particular pure supposition (εἰ + indicative), a general one (ἐάν + subjunctive, present in the conclusion), a future one (ἐάν + subjunctive, future in the conclusion), and a supposition contrary to fact: εἰ with a past indicative, and a past indicative with ἄν in the conclusion. The imperfect with ἄν points to present time (εἰ τυφλοὶ ἦτε, οὐκ ἂν εἴχετε ἁμαρτίαν); the aorist with ἄν to past time. Sometimes ἄν is omitted.",
          "example": "εἰ τυφλοὶ ἦτε, οὐκ ἂν εἴχετε ἁμαρτίαν",
          "notThis": "dropping ἄν from a contrary-to-fact conclusion, or mixing ἐάν with a past indicative",
          "useThis": "if you were blind, you would have no sin",
          "because": "Principles 34 and 45, Lessons XXXIII and XXXIX."
        },
        {
          "id": "l40-oume-inf",
          "title": "οὐ μή is a strong “never”; the infinitive may be an object; time may be dative",
          "rule": "οὐ μὴ πεινάσῃ, οὐ μὴ περιπατήσῃ: οὐ μή with the aorist subjunctive (or sometimes the future) is a strong denial of a future act. The infinitive often stands as the object of a verb of purpose or intention, and governs the same case as the other parts of the verb. The precise time at which something is done is in the dative: τῇ ἐσχάτῃ ἡμέρᾳ.",
          "example": "ὁ ἐρχόμενος πρός με οὐ μὴ πεινάσῃ",
          "notThis": "weakening οὐ μή to “perhaps not,” or putting the last day in the accusative as a direct object of time",
          "useThis": "he will certainly not hunger; on the last day",
          "because": "Principles 35–37 and 43, Lessons XXXIV and XXXVIII."
        },
        {
          "id": "l40-part-ga",
          "title": "Partitive genitive, genitive absolute, and δέ versus ἀλλά",
          "rule": "ἐκ τοῦ ὄχλου … ἔλεγον, some of the crowd were saying: a partitive genitive, with “some” omitted, may stand as the subject. A collective subject may take a plural predicate: ἐπάρατοί εἰσιν. τῆς ἑορτῆς μεσούσης, when the feast was midway, is a genitive absolute. δέ is a milder, postpositive “but”; ἀλλά marks the sharper contrast.",
          "example": "ἤδη δὲ τῆς ἑορτῆς μεσούσης ἀνέβη Ἰησοῦς εἰς τὸ ἱερόν",
          "notThis": "taking the feast as the subject of ἀνέβη, or treating every δέ as if it were as sharp as ἀλλά",
          "useThis": "when the feast was midway, Jesus went up; some of the crowd said…",
          "because": "Principles 40 and 42, and the notes on δέ and ἀλλά, Lessons XXXVI–XXXVII."
        },
        {
          "id": "l40-neg-q",
          "title": "οὐ denies a fact; μή a thought; questions and τοιοῦτος",
          "rule": "οὐ denies something as a matter of fact; μή denies it as a matter of thought (wishes, conditions, commands). The same difference appears in οὐδείς and μηδείς. A sentence question can be answered yes or no; οὐ in such a question expects yes, μή expects no. A word question (τίς, πῶς, πότε) cannot be answered yes or no. τοιοῦτος without the article is general, any such; with the article, particular, of such.",
          "example": "οὐδεὶς δύναται ἐλθεῖν πρός με ἐὰν μὴ ὁ πατὴρ … ἑλκύσῃ αὐτόν",
          "notThis": "using οὐ in a prohibition, or answering τίς ἥμαρτεν with a bare yes",
          "useThis": "οὐδείς, a fact; ἐὰν μή, a condition of thought; who sinned? is a word question",
          "because": "Lessons XXXV and XXXIX, Principles 44 and the notes on the two negatives."
        },
        {
          "id": "l40-thread",
          "title": "The thread of these chapters is life given, and sight given",
          "rule": "The man at the pool walks; the dead hear and live; he is the bread of life and the light of the world; living water is offered; a man born blind sees. The repeated claim is ἐγώ εἰμι, and the repeated call is to come, to believe, and to walk in the light. ἓν οἶδα ὅτι τυφλὸς ὢν ἄρτι βλέπω.",
          "example": "ἐγώ εἰμι τὸ φῶς τοῦ κόσμου· ὁ ἀκολουθῶν ἐμοὶ οὐ μὴ περιπατήσῃ ἐν τῇ σκοτίᾳ",
          "notThis": "treating chapters 5–9 as unconnected wonders, without the claim “I am” and the gift of life and sight",
          "useThis": "I am the light of the world; the one who follows me will not walk in darkness",
          "because": "The verses run from 5:8 and 5:24 through 6:35, 7:37, 8:12, 8:58, and 9:25."
        }
      ],
