      "patternHeading": "What the verses are doing",
      "patternText": "Before the feast, knowing that his hour has come, he rises from supper, washes the disciples’ feet, and wipes them with the towel. Peter says, οὐ μὴ νίψῃς μου τοὺς πόδας εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα. If I do not wash you, you have no part with me. If I, the Lord and the Teacher, washed your feet, you also ought to wash one another’s feet. ὑπόδειγμα γὰρ ἔδωκα ὑμῖν, I have given you an example. A new commandment I give you, that you love one another; as I have loved you, you also love one another. Connected sentences are co-ordinate when they are mutually independent. A compound sentence has a principal clause and a subordinate one. A subordinate clause that prepares the way is a protasis; the principal clause is the apodosis. Subordinate clauses are final, conditional, relative, causal, or of indirect discourse. After conditional clauses with εἰ, ἄν stands in the apodosis, with the imperfect indicative (present time) or the aorist indicative (past time); sometimes ἄν is omitted. In relative clauses after ὅς, ὅστις, ὅσος, ἄν is used mainly with the aorist subjunctive (future time) or the present subjunctive (continuous action).",
