          "id": "l44-clauses",
          "title": "Subordinate clauses are final, conditional, relative, causal, or reported speech",
          "rule": "Connected sentences are co-ordinate when they are mutually independent. A compound sentence consists of a principal and a subordinate clause. A subordinate clause that prepares the way for its principal clause is a protasis; the principal clause is the apodosis. Subordinate clauses are (1) final, of purpose, with ἵνα or ὅπως; (2) conditional, with εἰ or ἐάν; (3) relative, including temporal clauses; (4) causal, with ὅτι, διότι, ἐπεί, ἐπειδή, or ὡς; or (5) of indirect discourse, in which the substance of a quotation is given as a dependent sentence.",
          "example": "ὑπόδειγμα γὰρ ἔδωκα ὑμῖν ἵνα καθὼς ἐγὼ ἐποίησα ὑμῖν καὶ ὑμεῖς ποιῆτε",
          "notThis": "treating ἵνα ποιῆτε as a mere statement of fact, and not as a purpose clause",
          "useThis": "I gave you an example, in order that you also may do… (final ἵνα)",
