      "patternHeading": "What you have been reading",
      "patternText": "The Gospel is now a connected whole. In the beginning was the Word, and the Word became flesh. The Lamb of God; water become wine; a birth from above; God so loved the world; living water; a son who lives by a word. A man takes up his mat; he is the bread of life and the light of the world; a man born blind sees; the good shepherd lays down his life; Lazarus comes out; a grain of wheat falls and dies; a new commandment; I am the way and the true vine; I have overcome the world; what is truth?; it is finished; my Lord and my God; feed my sheep. These things have been written ἵνα πιστεύσητε ὅτι Ἰησοῦς ἐστιν ὁ χριστὸς ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ θεοῦ, καὶ ἵνα πιστεύοντες ζωὴν ἔχητε ἐν τῷ ὀνόματι αὐτοῦ. You have now seen, among much else, the cases and the article, the tenses and the moods, οὐ and μή, the four forms of condition, attraction of the relative, the articular infinitive, the genitive absolute, and the chief uses of the accusative, genitive, and dative.",
      "patternRows": [
        [
          "ἐν ἀρχῇ ἦν ὁ λόγος, καὶ ὁ λόγος ἦν πρὸς τὸν θεόν, καὶ θεὸς ἦν ὁ λόγος",
          "In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God."
        ],
        [
          "οὕτως γὰρ ἠγάπησεν ὁ θεὸς τὸν κόσμον ὥστε τὸν υἱὸν τὸν μονογενῆ ἔδωκεν",
          "For God so loved the world that he gave the only-begotten Son."
        ],
        [
          "ἐγώ εἰμι ἡ ὁδὸς καὶ ἡ ἀλήθεια καὶ ἡ ζωή",
          "I am the way and the truth and the life."
        ],
        [
          "ταῦτα δὲ γέγραπται ἵνα πιστεύσητε ὅτι Ἰησοῦς ἐστιν ὁ χριστὸς ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ θεοῦ",
          "These have been written that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God."
        ]
      ],
      "rules": [
        {
          "id": "l50-iam",
          "title": "The ‘I am’ lines gather the claim of the book",
          "rule": "The Word was God; I am the bread of life, the light of the world, the door, the good shepherd, the resurrection and the life, the way and the truth and the life, the true vine. Before Abraham came to be, I am. The signs, the hours, and the words all point to that claim, and to the life that is in his name.",
          "example": "ἐγώ εἰμι ἡ ὁδὸς καὶ ἡ ἀλήθεια καὶ ἡ ζωή",
          "notThis": "treating the “I am” lines as unconnected figures of speech, without the claim they make together",
          "useThis": "I am the way and the truth and the life",
          "because": "The lines run from 1:1 through 6:35, 8:12, 10:11, 11:25, 14:6, 15:5, and 8:58."
        },
        {
          "id": "l50-believe",
          "title": "The book is written that you may believe, and have life",
          "rule": "From the first disciples who came and saw, through the official who believed a word, Peter’s “to whom shall we go?,” the man who was blind and now sees, and Thomas’s “my Lord and my God,” the Gospel presses belief. These things have been written that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that believing you may have life in his name.",
          "example": "ταῦτα δὲ γέγραπται ἵνα πιστεύσητε ὅτι Ἰησοῦς ἐστιν ὁ χριστὸς ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ θεοῦ",
          "notThis": "as if the book were only a set of stories, without this purpose",
          "useThis": "these have been written that you may believe, and that believing you may have life",
          "because": "The wording is that of 20:31, read in the light of the whole Gospel."
        },
        {
          "id": "l50-attr-inf",
          "title": "The last principles: attraction, the articular infinitive, omitted antecedents",
          "rule": "The relative is often attracted into the case of its antecedent, especially from the accusative to the genitive, and also into a dative. When the antecedent would be a demonstrative, it is often omitted and implied in the relative. The infinitive with the article is an indeclinable neuter noun, in a case, sometimes after a preposition, and may be the subject or the object of a sentence, or may mark purpose or result.",
          "example": "τήρησον αὐτοὺς ἐν τῷ ὀνόματί σου ᾧ δέδωκάς μοι",
          "notThis": "writing a relative in the “logical” case every time, and never letting attraction or an implied antecedent stand",
          "useThis": "in your name, which you have given me (ᾧ, attracted); the articular infinitive as a noun",
          "because": "Principles 55–57, and the notes on the articular infinitive, Lessons XLV–XLVI."
        },
        {
          "id": "l50-cases",
          "title": "Accusative, genitive, and dative in these last chapters",
          "rule": "The accusative is the direct object, and also appears as cognate, of specification, of space and time, as a second object, or as a predicate accusative. The genitive may be objective (zeal for your house), or of relation (the door of the sheep). The dative marks the indirect object, interest, association and resemblance, and manner or instrument. A nominative with the article may stand for the vocative: ὁ κύριός μου καὶ ὁ θεός μου.",
          "example": "ὁ κύριός μου καὶ ὁ θεός μου",
          "notThis": "forcing every case into a single English “of” or “to,” without asking which use it is",
          "useThis": "My Lord and my God! (nominative with the article, in address); and the other case uses as the context shows",
          "because": "Principles 58–63, Lessons XLVII–XLVIII."
        },
        {
          "id": "l50-moods",
          "title": "Moods and commands, from the first lessons to the last",
          "rule": "The subjunctive exhorts (ἄγωμεν), forbids with μή (aorist), and deliberates (τί ποιῶμεν; τί εἴπω;). Negative commands take μή with the present imperative or the aorist subjunctive. The present imperative marks continuance; the aorist, a single act. Clauses with ἄν take the subjunctive. οὐ μή with the aorist subjunctive is a strong future denial. Contrary to fact has εἰ and a past indicative, with ἄν in the conclusion.",
          "example": "ἄγωμεν εἰς τὴν Ἰουδαίαν πάλιν",
          "notThis": "reading every subjunctive as a simple future, or every imperative as a past",
          "useThis": "let us go; do not…; whatever he may say; he will certainly not…; if you had been here…",
          "because": "Principles 33, 43, 49–54, and the earlier notes on οὐ, μή, and condition."
        },
        {
          "id": "l50-love",
          "title": "The thread is gift, glory, and love to the end",
          "rule": "The Word became flesh and we saw his glory. God so loved the world that he gave the only-begotten Son. A new commandment I give you, that you love one another; greater love has no one than this, that someone lay down his life for his friends. Feed my lambs; tend my sheep. There are also many other things which Jesus did; if they were all written, the world itself would not have room for the books.",
          "example": "ἐντολὴν καινὴν δίδωμι ὑμῖν ἵνα ἀγαπᾶτε ἀλλήλους",
          "notThis": "ending the book at the signs only, without the command to love and to feed",
          "useThis": "a new commandment I give you, that you love one another; feed my sheep",
          "because": "The verses are 1:14, 3:16, 13:34, 15:13, 21:15–17, and 21:25."
        }
      ],
